WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.299
In this video, we'll look at some of the core concepts of

2
00:00:04.299 --> 00:00:07.599
using OSC specifically inside Unreal Engine.

3
00:00:07.599 --> 00:00:11.380
The first step and the only step we need to take to start

4
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.220
using Open Sound Control inside Unreal Engine is to go

5
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:20.070
to the plugin tab and enable Open Sound Control plugin.

6
00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:24.230
You'll see a number of other plugins inside here that use OSC,

7
00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.109
which are not necessary for this course.

8
00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:30.030
Suffice to say they can be used in various environments such as

9
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.990
with virtual production or even something like Live Link Face,

10
00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.850
if you wanted to remotely trigger a capture session.

11
00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.429
Let's start with a simple scenario

12
00:00:38.429 --> 00:00:40.829
for Open Sound Control communication.

13
00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:42.670
It is a peer-to-peer protocol,

14
00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.670
which means we have one as a sender and one as a receiver.

15
00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.450
Let's start with this very basic scenario where we have

16
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.270
computer A and computer B,

17
00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:57.350
and computer A is trying to send message to computer B.

18
00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.829
Now let's look at what we're going to use

19
00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:02.270
it inside Unreal Engine projects.

20
00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:05.510
There are two key components we're going to use.

21
00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:09.709
One is OSC client and one is OSC server.

22
00:01:09.709 --> 00:01:11.750
For a OSC client,

23
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.709
OSC client is a node or

24
00:01:14.709 --> 00:01:18.310
a component we're going to use to send message,

25
00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.950
and an OSC server is a receiver which

26
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:26.549
listens to the OSC messages send it from the client.

27
00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:28.599
For those who are familiar with

28
00:01:28.599 --> 00:01:30.400
more traditional multiplayer setups

29
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.200
or using something like WebSockets,

30
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.959
this might seem a little bit inverted,

31
00:01:33.959 --> 00:01:37.159
which yes, we feel that way as well, so rest assured.

32
00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:38.760
Traditionally, you would have

33
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.000
a server who is sending messages to

34
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.840
a client who is receiving them but yes,

35
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.500
in this scenario for OSC,

36
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.400
you actually have the client who sends

37
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.810
messages to the server who receives them.

38
00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:55.689
Now let's look back to our OSC sample scenario,

39
00:01:55.689 --> 00:02:00.049
where computer A tries to send a message to computer B.

40
00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:02.930
Now we said that the computer A as

41
00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:08.289
the sender has an IP A.A.A.A and computer B as the receiver,

42
00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:12.250
it has the IP of B.B.B.B,

43
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:17.810
and it has port BBBBB forwarded to computer B.

44
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.849
So now on computer A,

45
00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:21.650
computer A wants to create

46
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.889
an OSC client targeting computer B.

47
00:02:24.889 --> 00:02:28.289
In this node, the send IP address,

48
00:02:28.289 --> 00:02:30.710
we're going to put in computer B's address,

49
00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:34.689
B.B.B.B, and we want to specify that we're going to

50
00:02:34.689 --> 00:02:38.090
send this message through the port BBBBB,

51
00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.650
where we know it's already opened on B's side.

52
00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.960
Right, and just noting of course that

53
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.360
IP addresses and ports are almost always,

54
00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.879
to my knowledge, numbers,

55
00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:50.919
we're just using letters here to be illustrative.

56
00:02:50.919 --> 00:02:53.479
Also note that even if you're working on

57
00:02:53.479 --> 00:02:56.759
a local area network where everyone's on the same subnet,

58
00:02:56.759 --> 00:02:59.000
you don't actually need to open any ports,

59
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.479
but you do need to put a port number in here to match

60
00:03:02.479 --> 00:03:04.080
across the server and client

61
00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.620
or the message will not successfully send.

62
00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.840
Also, the client must have a unique name as does the server.

63
00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.680
Don't go ahead and just name

64
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.919
your server computer and then name your client computer,

65
00:03:16.919 --> 00:03:19.199
and then all clients also need a unique name.

66
00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:22.139
You can call them client one, client two, etc.

67
00:03:22.139 --> 00:03:23.900
But if anything is duplicated,

68
00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.520
then the message will also not successfully send,

69
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.699
and you won't have a very useful error message

70
00:03:29.699 --> 00:03:31.419
informing you of the reason why.

71
00:03:31.419 --> 00:03:32.580
As you might be able to tell,

72
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.500
we have run into this issue before.

73
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.280
Computer B as a receiver,

74
00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.479
in order to receive the messages from Computer A,

75
00:03:40.479 --> 00:03:43.639
Computer B will need a OSCE server.

76
00:03:43.639 --> 00:03:47.919
On Computer B, that's used to create OSCE server node,

77
00:03:47.919 --> 00:03:50.840
and inside this node,

78
00:03:51.039 --> 00:03:54.560
we have to put in the receive IP address and the port.

79
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.599
The receive IP address,

80
00:03:56.599 --> 00:04:00.080
we don't need to specify who we're receiving it from.

81
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.960
OSCE server is just a representation of Computer B,

82
00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:08.159
so it's there and listening to any coming messages.

83
00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:10.219
In the receive IP address,

84
00:04:10.219 --> 00:04:13.560
we can either put in Computer B's IP,

85
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.540
in this case, we use b.b.b.b as a representation,

86
00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.800
or we can just leave it blank,

87
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.120
and it will by default try to use the first IP address

88
00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.000
it can got from the computer's system.

89
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.399
And in the port,

90
00:04:29.399 --> 00:04:33.759
we're going to put in the open port for Computer B.

91
00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:37.399
So in our case, we use b.b.b.b to show it,

92
00:04:37.399 --> 00:04:39.839
but in reality, remember,

93
00:04:39.839 --> 00:04:44.720
it must be a number-based port.

94
00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.360
So once we put into those two information,

95
00:04:48.399 --> 00:04:49.839
this OSCE server will know

96
00:04:49.839 --> 00:04:53.560
that it is representing the Computer B address,

97
00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:58.560
and it is listening to any OSCE message

98
00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:01.339
coming from port b.b.b.b.

99
00:05:01.339 --> 00:05:03.680
And by default, the start listening will be checked,

100
00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.040
but just double check to make sure it is checked,

101
00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.259
so it is listening to the coming messages.

102
00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:11.040
And same as OSCE client,

103
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.639
we need to give the server a unique name.

104
00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:17.199
On this slide, we're showing some of the additional nodes

105
00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.759
that you have access to when you're sending an OSCE message.

106
00:05:20.759 --> 00:05:22.720
And rest assured, we'll go over all of these

107
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.279
in more detail, but just note, for example,

108
00:05:25.279 --> 00:05:27.540
the center column where we're seeing values

109
00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.199
like add float, add string, add bool, add integer.

110
00:05:31.199 --> 00:05:33.800
This should give you a sense of the wide variety

111
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.279
of different kinds of messages

112
00:05:35.279 --> 00:05:37.839
that can be sent using OSCE.

113
00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:40.319
And now let's look how a receiver side

114
00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:42.839
is getting those messages.

115
00:05:42.839 --> 00:05:45.300
So on the receiver side,

116
00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.879
we can use get OSCE message flow at index,

117
00:05:48.879 --> 00:05:51.420
get OSCE message string at index,

118
00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:55.259
get bool at index, and et cetera.

119
00:05:55.259 --> 00:05:58.040
So we can see that we can use these nodes

120
00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.319
to get different kinds of values

121
00:06:00.319 --> 00:06:02.019
and from different orders.

122
00:06:02.019 --> 00:06:06.339
So say we send a message with the first one is a float

123
00:06:06.339 --> 00:06:08.259
and the second one is a string,

124
00:06:08.259 --> 00:06:10.060
we can imagine that we're going to start

125
00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:11.379
to combine things together,

126
00:06:11.379 --> 00:06:13.899
and we get the float at index zero,

127
00:06:13.899 --> 00:06:17.259
and we get a string at index one, et cetera.

128
00:06:17.259 --> 00:06:20.839
So this is a way we can start to create

129
00:06:20.839 --> 00:06:25.540
and receive a more complicated OSCE message structure.

130
00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.980
And note that we're using an event delegate here.

131
00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:29.699
Again, we'll go over this in more detail,

132
00:06:29.699 --> 00:06:31.699
but this would be the traditional way

133
00:06:31.699 --> 00:06:37.009
to make sure that the correct messages are being received.

134
00:06:37.009 --> 00:06:41.990
Now let's see how to structure a OSCE message.

135
00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:45.569
Now let's say computer A wants to send a float

136
00:06:45.569 --> 00:06:50.569
to computer B with the address slash test slash send float.

137
00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:56.199
And the value of the float will be 0.35.

138
00:06:56.199 --> 00:06:58.600
Now the first step is we want to make

139
00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.600
an empty OSCE message.

140
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:04.720
As you can see here, this is the make OSCE message.

141
00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:05.939
This will be an empty one.

142
00:07:05.939 --> 00:07:08.360
We can start to fill things inside.

143
00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:12.000
So first thing we want to set the address.

144
00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.560
Inside Unreal, we can use this node,

145
00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:17.040
convert string to OSCE address,

146
00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.420
and we can type in our string,

147
00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:21.360
or actually it will be our address here,

148
00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.339
slash test slash send float.

149
00:07:24.339 --> 00:07:25.600
Now we have this message.

150
00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.360
The output of this message will be the same OSCE message,

151
00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:32.980
but now with the correct address.

152
00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.220
So we get this output,

153
00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.819
and we add a float to this OSCE message,

154
00:07:38.819 --> 00:07:42.850
and our value would be 0.35.

155
00:07:43.649 --> 00:07:46.569
Now we still continue to grab the output of this message,

156
00:07:46.569 --> 00:07:49.449
and we want to grab our OSCE client,

157
00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:51.810
which is now targeting computer B.

158
00:07:55.139 --> 00:07:57.500
So we will send this message

159
00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:01.439
to our computer B OSCE client here

160
00:08:01.439 --> 00:08:04.699
with a send OSCE message node.

161
00:08:04.699 --> 00:08:07.899
And finally, we want to clear the OSCE message

162
00:08:07.899 --> 00:08:09.379
just like a best practice.

163
00:08:09.379 --> 00:08:11.779
So we make sure the next one we're sending

164
00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:13.819
has a clear start.

165
00:08:13.819 --> 00:08:16.699
Right, and we can either make an OSCE message

166
00:08:16.699 --> 00:08:18.060
like we have here at the beginning,

167
00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:21.180
or this can be a variable that is saved and reused.

168
00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:23.540
I will just say you should be careful about that

169
00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:27.620
because it's very easy to accidentally resend old data,

170
00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:28.959
which is why it can be useful

171
00:08:28.959 --> 00:08:31.839
to make a fresh OSCE message each time.

172
00:08:31.839 --> 00:08:35.139
Also, please note that at the beginning of the string,

173
00:08:35.139 --> 00:08:37.419
when you're converting to an OSCE address,

174
00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:39.379
that that front slash is very important.

175
00:08:39.379 --> 00:08:43.019
It will not work without that first slash.

176
00:08:43.019 --> 00:08:45.279
Now let's move on to computer B.

177
00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:48.779
So now we know computer A already sent a float value

178
00:08:48.779 --> 00:08:51.419
of 0.35 to computer B,

179
00:08:51.419 --> 00:08:53.740
and B wants to receive this message,

180
00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:56.059
and we will use this float value

181
00:08:56.059 --> 00:09:01.059
to change an actor scale according to the received float.

182
00:09:01.179 --> 00:09:04.860
So now let's see how to structure this on the B side.

183
00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:08.990
So first, we will drag our OSCE server,

184
00:09:09.029 --> 00:09:13.509
which we already created as a representation of computer B.

185
00:09:13.509 --> 00:09:17.909
It has continued to listen to the coming OSCE message

186
00:09:17.909 --> 00:09:20.710
from port BBBBB.

187
00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:24.590
And now we know we want to find an OSCE message

188
00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:29.590
with the specific address called slash test slash send float.

189
00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.750
So we will start with this node,

190
00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.789
convert string to OSCE address.

191
00:09:34.789 --> 00:09:35.870
And inside the string,

192
00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:40.600
we will put in the OSCE message address.

193
00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:45.600
And we want to bind an event to our OSCE server here,

194
00:09:45.639 --> 00:09:48.779
which, so this event is called bind event

195
00:09:48.779 --> 00:09:52.700
to on OSCE address pattern matches path.

196
00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:56.679
So it will continue to listen to new OSCE messages

197
00:09:56.679 --> 00:10:00.740
and it will filter and check if there's any OSCE message

198
00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.639
with the same address we set here.

199
00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:07.059
If it matches, it will trigger this custom event

200
00:10:07.059 --> 00:10:09.919
to fire here.

201
00:10:09.919 --> 00:10:12.980
And now when this event is triggered,

202
00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:16.779
we want to get the float value from this message.

203
00:10:16.779 --> 00:10:19.899
The way to get it will be drag out

204
00:10:19.899 --> 00:10:22.500
from this message output here.

205
00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:24.000
And we will use the node,

206
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:27.100
get OSCE message float at index.

207
00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:30.500
In our case, we know we want to get the index zero,

208
00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:32.100
the first one.

209
00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:36.019
So we use this node and it will output a value.

210
00:10:36.019 --> 00:10:38.679
And we know we want to change an actor scale

211
00:10:38.679 --> 00:10:40.179
using this float value.

212
00:10:40.179 --> 00:10:43.940
So we will just hook into set actor scale 3D

213
00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:48.940
and we will convert the float value into a vector 3D.

214
00:10:49.059 --> 00:10:51.340
On this slide, we're looking at how to construct

215
00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:53.740
an OSCE bundle, which allows you to send

216
00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:56.419
as many OSCE messages as you'd like.

217
00:10:56.419 --> 00:10:58.259
In the example we're demonstrating here,

218
00:10:58.259 --> 00:11:02.080
we're showing how to send an XYZ coordinate value.

219
00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.360
So you can see where we have convert string to OSCE address.

220
00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:10.360
We're using the pathing of float X, float Y, float Z,

221
00:11:10.779 --> 00:11:13.639
and all of that gets added into OSCE messages,

222
00:11:13.639 --> 00:11:16.600
which are then added into a single bundle.

223
00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:18.779
The one OSCE bundle is sent.

224
00:11:18.779 --> 00:11:20.740
We clear it, clear the messages,

225
00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:23.139
and then can once again send this again.

226
00:11:23.139 --> 00:11:25.919
So this is not unlike the way that you would replicate

227
00:11:25.919 --> 00:11:29.559
the transform values of say a player in a scene,

228
00:11:29.559 --> 00:11:30.700
but instead of doing this

229
00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:32.320
in a traditional multiplayer setting,

230
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:35.620
we can do it entirely with OSCE signals.

231
00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:36.539
In the next video,

232
00:11:36.539 --> 00:11:38.940
we'll look at how to configure your firewall

233
00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:40.379
and set a port forwarding

234
00:11:40.379 --> 00:11:43.100
to ensure that OSCE works as expected.
